// `MatMul` — rank-2-*specific*, deliberately not pack-generic like `Ring`/
// `Index` in `tensor.cleave`: matmul contracts one *specific* dimension
// (`Tensor<T,N,M>`'s own trailing `M` against `Tensor<T,M,K>`'s own leading
// `M`) — a real, individually-named relationship between two operands' own
// dimensions, not "however many dims, treated uniformly" the way
// elementwise ops or a flat index array are. `N`/`M`/`K` stay ordinary,
// individually-declared const generics; `Tensor<T,N,M>` is just an
// ordinary two-generic instantiation of `tensor.cleave`'s own pack-generic
// struct — ordinary generic-struct-reference machinery, unrelated to any
// of that file's own pack work, resolves it ordinarily.
//
// `+`/`*` on scalars resolve against the real stdlib `Ring` (always in the
// prelude) — no algebra declared for `matmul` shares a name with `mul`,
// and deliberately so: `algebras_with_fn` picks a candidate by name+arity
// alone, with no shape disambiguation at that stage (see `infer_call`,
// `registry.rs`'s `algebras_with_fn`) — a second algebra also declaring a
// 2-arg `mul` would make *every* `*` in a program ambiguous, including a
// genuinely scalar one. No qualified-call syntax exists yet to
// disambiguate (`doc/backlog.md`, "Qualified-call syntax"), so matrix
// multiplication is an ordinary named method, `matmul`, called directly —
// not wired to `*`.
algebra MatMul<A, B, C> {
    fn matmul(a: A, b: B) -> C;
    derivative matmul(a, b): add(matmul(d(a), b), matmul(a, d(b)));
    // The real linear-algebra adjoint of matrix multiplication: for
    // `Z = H*W` (`H: R^{n x m}`, `W: R^{m x k}`), `dH = dZ*W^T`, `dW = H^T*dZ`
    // — the two transposes are the two adjoints of the same bilinear map,
    // one per argument (`⟨Av,w⟩ = ⟨v,A^*w⟩`, `A^* = A^T` for a real matrix
    // under the standard inner product). Needs `Transpose<A,B>` (just
    // below) — unlike the `derivative` rule above (which recurses through
    // `d(a)`/`d(b)` and never itself needs a transpose), the reverse-mode
    // rule must physically reorient each operand to route the upstream
    // adjoint `u` back through the correct contracted dimension.
    //
    // Routed through `matmul_transpose_a`/`_b` (just below), not a real
    // `transpose(...)` call feeding an ordinary `matmul`: `dH = dZ*W^T`,
    // `dW = H^T*dZ` never actually need a materialized transpose at all —
    // MLIR's own `indexing_maps` mechanism reads the "transposed" operand
    // with an ordinary contiguous load instead (`mlir_lower.rs::build_
    // matmul_transpose_no_seed`'s own doc comment has the full derivation,
    // including the real, confirmed `linalg.transpose` gather/scatter cost
    // this specifically avoids, and the melior Properties-API gap this had
    // to work around to build correctly — `doc/backlog.md`).
    adjoint matmul(a, b), u: (matmul_transpose_b(u, b), matmul_transpose_a(a, u));

    // A user (or a later-derived expression) writing a transpose directly
    // into a `matmul` operand, literally — not through the `adjoint` rule
    // above, which already routes straight to `matmul_transpose_a/b`
    // without ever going through a real `transpose(...)` node at all — gets
    // rewritten to the identical zero-copy form: `matmul_transpose_a`/`_b`
    // read the "transposed" operand via `indexing_maps` (an ordinary
    // contiguous load), never materializing a real `linalg.transpose`
    // (this file's own `MatMulTransposeA`/`MatMulTransposeB` doc comments
    // above have the full mechanism). Equality saturation, not a rewrite
    // committed to eagerly: both forms coexist in the same e-class, cost-
    // based extraction picks the cheaper one — safe under today's plain
    // node-count cost function specifically because this rule can only
    // ever reduce or hold node count, never increase it (2 nodes either
    // side: `matmul(transpose(a), b)` vs `matmul_transpose_a(a, b)`).
    axiom matmul_transpose_a_rewrite(a, b): matmul(transpose(a), b) == matmul_transpose_a(a, b);
    axiom matmul_transpose_b_rewrite(a, b): matmul(a, transpose(b)) == matmul_transpose_b(a, b);

    // `transpose(A*B) = B^T*A^T` — a real, always-true linear-algebra
    // identity, deliberately declared in *this* direction only, not as a
    // bidirectional `==` a saturation pass would explore both ways: unlike
    // the two rules just above, whether the *other* direction (materializing
    // a real `transpose(matmul(a,b))` into two separate transposes) is ever
    // a genuine win depends on real operation cost (domain-specific, per the
    // conversation that raised this — graphics workloads hit this pattern
    // for exactly the row/col-order reasons that motivate it there), which
    // today's plain node-count `DerivativeFreeCost` cannot judge at all (it
    // would even prefer the *wrong* direction, since two transposes plus a
    // matmul is structurally more nodes than one transpose plus a matmul).
    // *This* direction needs no such judgment call: `matmul(transpose(b),
    // transpose(a))` (3 nodes) reducing to `transpose(matmul(a, b))` (2
    // nodes) is a node-count win unconditionally, so it's sound to declare
    // even under today's cost function — the reverse stays undeclared until
    // a real, shape-aware cost model exists to arbitrate it.
    axiom matmul_transpose_distributes(a, b): matmul(transpose(b), transpose(a)) == transpose(matmul(a, b));

    // `A@B + C` — real accumulation into an explicit, already-computed `c`
    // (`Fma<A,B,C>`, below), fusing to one `linalg.matmul` seeded from `c`
    // directly (`mlir_lower.rs::build_matmul_add`'s own doc comment) — no
    // `linalg.fill` zero-fill, no separate elementwise `add` pass. `cblas_
    // sgemm` already computes exactly this (`alpha*A@B + beta*C`) for free
    // once a real BLAS dispatch reaches here (`doc/plan-blas-native.md`
    // §7 — deliberately not wired to `stdlib/blas/blas.cleave`'s own
    // `Sgemm::sgemm` yet: native lowering first, a real oracle, *then* a
    // size threshold, the same discipline `MatMul::matmul` itself is still
    // owed for its own BLAS dispatch).
    //
    // Declared *here*, inside `MatMul` (not inside `Fma` itself, despite
    // `Fma::fma` being this rule's own RHS) — deliberately, not a style
    // choice: `egraph.rs::axiom_rewrites` only ever considers an axiom
    // whose *home* algebra's own method was genuinely, statically reached
    // by a real call site somewhere in the function being optimized
    // (`fwd.reached`, captured once, *before* any rewriting — never grown
    // mid-saturation as new e-nodes appear). `Fma::fma` is never called
    // directly by any real source in this codebase (only ever *introduced*
    // by this very axiom firing) — homing the rule there would mean it can
    // never be found at all, a real, confirmed dead end (`doc/backlog.md`'s
    // own note has the two failed placements tried first). `MatMul::matmul`
    // is what's genuinely reached instead (a caller writes `matmul(a,b) +
    // c`, an ordinary call), exactly the same reasoning that already places
    // `matmul_transpose_a_rewrite`/`_b_rewrite` above inside `MatMul` too,
    // even though *their* own RHS names a different algebra's method.
    //
    // The two transpose variants just below are for the identical reason
    // written against the fully *unfused* nested form (`matmul(transpose(
    // a), b)`, not `matmul_transpose_a(a, b)`) — a second axiom keyed on
    // `MatMulTransposeA`'s own reached types would hit the exact same dead
    // end `fma_rewrite`'s original `Fma`-homed attempt did: `matmul_
    // transpose_a` is itself never statically reached either, only ever
    // introduced by `matmul_transpose_a_rewrite` firing in this *same*
    // saturation pass — `axiom_rewrites` builds its whole rule set once,
    // upfront, from the pre-saturation reached set, so a rule can never
    // "come online" mid-saturation just because some other rule already
    // fired. One direct, single-hop rule from the fully naive form to the
    // fully fused target is what actually reaches it — `matmul_transpose_
    // a_rewrite` still fires too, on the same term, since both rules share
    // `MatMul` as their home and are therefore both in the same rule set,
    // but the *fused* target here is reached by this rule alone, not by
    // chaining through the other one.
    axiom fma_rewrite(a, b, c): add(matmul(a, b), c) == fma(a, b, c);
    axiom fma_transpose_a_rewrite(a, b, c): add(matmul(transpose(a), b), c) == fma_transpose_a(a, b, c);
    axiom fma_transpose_b_rewrite(a, b, c): add(matmul(a, transpose(b)), c) == fma_transpose_b(a, b, c);
}

// `dH = dZ*W^T` — `Transpose<B,?>` is never involved: `u: Tensor<T,N,K>`
// (`dZ`, the upstream adjoint) times `b: Tensor<T,M,K>` (`W`, read as if
// transposed) gives `Tensor<T,N,M>` (`dH`'s own shape), matching `MatMul`'s
// own `adjoint`'s first return slot exactly.
algebra MatMulTransposeB<A, B, C> {
    fn matmul_transpose_b(a: A, b: B) -> C;
}

impl<T: Float, const P: i32, const Q: i32, const R: i32>
    MatMulTransposeB<Tensor<T,P,Q>, Tensor<T,R,Q>, Tensor<T,P,R>> {
    fn matmul_transpose_b(a, b) {
        mlir::linalg::matmul_transpose_b(a, b)
    }
}

// `dW = H^T*dZ` — `a: Tensor<T,N,M>` (`H`, read as if transposed) times
// `u: Tensor<T,N,K>` (`dZ`) gives `Tensor<T,M,K>` (`dW`'s own shape),
// matching `MatMul`'s own `adjoint`'s second return slot exactly.
algebra MatMulTransposeA<A, B, C> {
    fn matmul_transpose_a(a: A, b: B) -> C;
}

impl<T: Float, const P: i32, const Q: i32, const R: i32>
    MatMulTransposeA<Tensor<T,P,Q>, Tensor<T,P,R>, Tensor<T,Q,R>> {
    fn matmul_transpose_a(a, b) {
        mlir::linalg::matmul_transpose_a(a, b)
    }
}

// The adjoint's own real dependency, not matmul's: `A: R^{n x m} -> A^T:
// R^{m x n}`. Deliberately its own algebra, rank-2-specific like `MatMul`
// itself (not pack-generic like `Ring`/`Index` in `tensor.cleave`) — a
// transpose permutes two *specific*, individually-named dimensions, the
// same reason `MatMul` itself isn't pack-generic either (see its own doc
// comment above).
algebra Transpose<A, B> {
    fn transpose(a: A) -> B;

    // `(A^T)^T = A` — always true, always a strict node-count win either
    // direction (1 node either side), unlike `matmul`'s own `transpose(a*b)`
    // distribution above (deliberately one-directional there, needing a
    // real cost model this project doesn't have yet for the *other*
    // direction) — no such caution needed here.
    axiom transpose_transpose(a): transpose(transpose(a)) == a;

    // Transpose is linear: `A^T + B^T = (A+B)^T`, `A^T - B^T = (A-B)^T` —
    // always true, and, like `matmul`'s own distribution rule above (not
    // `transpose_transpose`'s own symmetric case), only safe to declare in
    // the *collapsing* direction: `add(transpose(a), transpose(b))` (3
    // nodes: `add` + two `transpose`) is more nodes than `transpose(add(a,
    // b))` (2 nodes) unconditionally, a real node-count win regardless of
    // today's plain `DerivativeFreeCost` — the *other* direction (pushing
    // one `transpose` into two) would need the same real, shape-aware cost
    // model this project doesn't have yet, exactly like `matmul`'s own
    // undeclared reverse direction, so it stays undeclared here too.
    // `Ring<T>`'s own `add`/`sub` — single-generic, not `Transpose`'s own
    // multi-target concern, resolved the identical, already-working way
    // any cross-algebra reference to a single-generic algebra always has.
    axiom transpose_add_distributes(a, b): add(transpose(a), transpose(b)) == transpose(add(a, b));
    axiom transpose_sub_distributes(a, b): sub(transpose(a), transpose(b)) == transpose(sub(a, b));
}

impl<T: Float, const N: i32, const M: i32> Transpose<Tensor<T,N,M>, Tensor<T,M,N>> {
    fn transpose(a) {
        // No seed needed at all — `linalg.transpose` has no reduction
        // dimension (a pure permutation, every output element touched
        // exactly once), so `mlir_lower.rs::build_transpose_no_seed` builds
        // its own destination internally (`tensor.empty()`, genuinely
        // uninitialized) and a payload region that never reads it
        // (`linalg.yield` the input straight through) — see that function's
        // own doc comment for the full story, including why a `Ring::
        // zero()`-seeded accumulate used to sit here and is no longer
        // needed. `permutation: "array<i64: 1, 0>"` is the only 2D
        // permutation this impl needs — swap the two axes.
        mlir::linalg::transpose(a, permutation: "array<i64: 1, 0>")
    }
}

// `A@B + C` — real accumulation into an explicit, already-computed `c`,
// not a fresh zero-seeded destination. `cblas_sgemm` already computes
// exactly this (`alpha*A@B + beta*C`) for free once a real BLAS dispatch
// exists here (`doc/plan-blas-native.md` §7 — `stdlib/blas/blas.cleave`'s
// own `Sgemm::sgemm`, deliberately not wired to this yet: native lowering
// first, a real oracle, *then* a size threshold, same discipline `MatMul::
// matmul` itself is still owed for its own BLAS dispatch). Native lowering
// (below) needs nothing beyond `linalg.matmul`'s own already-documented
// `C := A@B + C` semantics — see `mlir_lower.rs::build_matmul_add`'s own
// doc comment.
//
// Deliberately its own algebra (not a second method on `MatMul`, same
// reasoning as `MatMulTransposeA`/`MatMulTransposeB` above) — an impl
// block only ever exposes its own algebra's required method to ordinary
// call resolution.
//
// No `derivative`/`adjoint` rule declared here, and deliberately so: a
// `grad`/`derive` request differentiates the CPS form *before* the axiom
// pass below ever runs (`pipeline.rs::build_optimized_cps`'s own call
// order — `build_cps_program`, which includes `synthesize_derivatives`,
// strictly precedes `optimize_program`), so it only ever sees the
// unfused `matmul(a,b) + c` a caller actually wrote, using `MatMul`'s own
// already-declared `derivative`/`adjoint` rules — `fma` never needs to be
// differentiable itself, only ever introduced *after* differentiation has
// already happened. Same posture `MatMulTransposeA`/`MatMulTransposeB`
// themselves already have (no `derivative`/`adjoint` of their own either).
//
// The actual fusion axioms (`fma_rewrite`/`fma_transpose_a_rewrite`/
// `fma_transpose_b_rewrite`) live inside `MatMul`'s own algebra body
// above, not here — see that algebra's own comment on `fma_rewrite` for
// exactly why (in short: an axiom is only ever considered when its *home*
// algebra's own method was genuinely, statically reached by a real call
// site, and `fma` itself is never called directly anywhere — only ever
// introduced by those rules firing).
algebra Fma<A, B, C> {
    fn fma(a: A, b: B, c: C) -> C;
}

algebra FmaTransposeA<A, B, C> {
    fn fma_transpose_a(a: A, b: B, c: C) -> C;
}

algebra FmaTransposeB<A, B, C> {
    fn fma_transpose_b(a: A, b: B, c: C) -> C;
}

impl<T: Float, const N: i32, const M: i32, const K: i32>
    Fma<Tensor<T,N,M>, Tensor<T,M,K>, Tensor<T,N,K>> {
    fn fma(a, b, c) {
        mlir::linalg::matmul_add(a, b, c)
    }
}

impl<T: Float, const P: i32, const Q: i32, const R: i32>
    FmaTransposeA<Tensor<T,P,Q>, Tensor<T,P,R>, Tensor<T,Q,R>> {
    fn fma_transpose_a(a, b, c) {
        mlir::linalg::matmul_transpose_a_add(a, b, c)
    }
}

impl<T: Float, const P: i32, const Q: i32, const R: i32>
    FmaTransposeB<Tensor<T,P,Q>, Tensor<T,R,Q>, Tensor<T,P,R>> {
    fn fma_transpose_b(a, b, c) {
        mlir::linalg::matmul_transpose_b_add(a, b, c)
    }
}

impl<T: Float, const N: i32, const M: i32, const K: i32>
    MatMul<Tensor<T,N,M>, Tensor<T,M,K>, Tensor<T,N,K>> {
    // `doc/backlog.md`'s own "`--inline` itself, directly confirmed as the
    // real cause of the `l1` register spill" entry has the full story of why
    // `#[no_inline]` is deliberately NOT applied here, despite a real,
    // general, working `no_inline` unit-attribute mechanism existing end to
    // end (`mlir_lower.rs`) and despite a real, confirmed, isolated win on
    // this exact reduction (`K=784`, zero allocation calls inside the hot
    // loop, a fully register-resident accumulator). Measured, properly, in
    // a real low-noise terminal, both ways, same machine, same binary: the
    // *full* kernel is ~6-8% *slower* with it than without, because the
    // attribute is declared on the whole generic impl -- every one of this
    // project's eight real call sites gets it, not just the one (`l1`'s own
    // forward matmul) the register-spill problem was ever confirmed on --
    // and `dps_rewrite.rs`'s own copy-elimination breaks for all eight,
    // once per layer's own weight-update struct-field write
    // (`stdlib/nn/nn.cleave`'s own `Dense`-level `Optimizer::step`).
    //
    // The real root cause, found by following the *allocation* angle
    // instead (the same backlog entry): the spill isn't really about this
    // matmul at all -- it's the x86-64 calling convention itself, forced to
    // spill every live vector register across *any* call a callee might
    // clobber, and `net_grad` (this reduction's own real caller, in the
    // actual kernel) makes plenty of them -- every intermediate tensor
    // allocation in that function still goes through a genuine heap
    // `cleave_alloc_rc`, never the arena (`cleave_alloc_local`) `region_
    // analysis.rs` already, correctly, proves this exact function safe to
    // use, because that verdict only ever gates *struct* construction
    // today, never a tensor value. `#[no_inline]` only ever treated the
    // symptom (isolate the one reduction from whichever nearby call happens
    // to force the spill); the real fix -- routing bufferization's own
    // tensor allocation through the arena inside a region-local function --
    // is scoped precisely in the backlog but not yet built.
    fn matmul(a, b) {
        // No seed needed at all any more, and deliberately so — this impl
        // used to build one here (`let init: Tensor<T,N,K> = Ring::zero();`,
        // still worth knowing about: `tensor.empty()`, uninitialized memory,
        // was tried *first*, and was a real, if long-latent, correctness
        // bug — `linalg.matmul` is documented as `C := A@B + C`, BLAS GEMM
        // semantics, and genuinely *loads* its own accumulator's current
        // value before adding into it, confirmed directly via `--dump-mlir-
        // lowered`; `matmul(a, Ring::zero())` gave tiny nonzero noise
        // instead of exactly `a` unchanged the moment a real allocation
        // pattern shift meant `tensor.empty()`'s own backing memory was no
        // longer accidentally zero). `Ring::zero()` fixed that correctly,
        // but cost a real, measured `memset` on every single call (VTune,
        // `examples/mnist-interop`: ~9.7% of wall time, `Dense<f32,784,
        // 512>`-sized zero-fills on every forward pass) — a real cost, not
        // a redundant copy `dps_rewrite.rs` could just elide the way it
        // does for a plain overwrite. `mlir_lower.rs::build_matmul_no_seed`
        // now builds this op directly (a `linalg.generic`, not the *named*
        // `linalg.matmul` this call used to become) whose own payload
        // region only ever reads the destination's *own* value on the
        // branch it never selects — see that function's own doc comment
        // for the full mechanism and why it's mathematically identical to
        // the zero-seeded version, at a fraction of the cost.
        mlir::linalg::matmul(a, b)
    }
}
